window.docContents[7].resolve({"/Connectivity/#section-connected-graphs":{"contents":"The two notions differ only for the graph on an empty vertex type. This graph is\nvacuously preconnected, but it is not connected. With this convention a graph is\nconnected exactly when it has precisely one connected component; the graph with no\nvertices has none.\n\nexample (h : G.Connected) {u v : V} : G.Reachable u v := h.preconnected u v\n\n\nexample (h : G.Connected) : Nonempty V := h.nonempty\n\n\nexample : (⊥ : SimpleGraph Empty).Preconnected := fun u => u.elim\nexample : ¬ (⊥ : SimpleGraph Empty).Connected := fun h => h.nonempty.some.elim\n\n\nNotice that rather than having to write h.preconnected u v for G.Reachable u v\n(where h : G.Connected) there is a CoeFun instance that lets us write the more\nsuccinct h u v:\n\nexample (h : G.Connected) {u v : V} : G.Reachable u v := h u v\n\n\nThe hypercube is connected: we can use the Hamming path to walk between any two\nvertices, and we already gave a Nonempty instance for its vertices which Lean will\nfind automatically.\n\nlemma connected_hypercube (n : ℕ) : Q[n].Connected where\n  preconnected := fun u v => ⟨u.hammingPath v⟩\n\n\nFor a concrete finite graph with decidable adjacency, connectivity is decidable, so\ndecide can check it (see Evaluating and Deciding).\n\nexample : C[5].Connected := by decide\n\n\nWe have already met one source of connected graphs: a Hamiltonian graph is connected,\nsince a Hamiltonian cycle passes through every vertex\n(Hamiltonian Paths and Cycles).\n\n#check IsHamiltonian.connected\n\n\nA connected graph has a path between any two vertices.\n\n#check Connected.exists_isPath\n\n\nA graph is connected iff there is a vertex v from which every other vertex is\nreachable.\n\n#check connected_iff_exists_forall_reachable\n\n\nIn the Mycielskian of a graph G, any vertex u that is not isolated in G has\nboth its left and right copies in mμ(G) connected to none. Given the edge u — v\nin G we have the walks uʳ — none and uˡ — vʳ — none in mμ(G). Conversely\nif u is an isolated vertex in G then uˡ is an isolated vertex in mμ(G).\n\nHence mμ(G) is connected iff G has no isolated vertices.\n\ntheorem connected_mycielskian_iff : mμ(G).Connected ↔ ∀ v, ¬ G.IsIsolated v := by\n  simp only [IsIsolated, not_forall, not_not]\n  constructor <;> intro h\n  · intro v\n    obtain ⟨p⟩ := h.preconnected vˡ none\n    cases p; grind [mycielskian_adj]\n  · apply (connected_iff_exists_forall_reachable _).2 ⟨none, fun z => ?_⟩\n    rcases z with (_ | ⟨v | v⟩)\n    · rfl\n    · obtain ⟨u, h⟩ := h v\n      exact (by simp : mμ(G).Adj _ _).reachable.trans\n            (by simpa using h.symm : mμ(G).Adj uʳ _).reachable\n    · exact (by simp : mμ(G).Adj _ _).reachable\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Connectivity\u0009Connectivity","header":"10.1.1. Connected Graphs","id":"/Connectivity/#section-connected-graphs"},"/Decidability___-Finiteness-and-Degrees/#section-decidability-and-kernel-reduction":{"contents":"We already provided decidable adjacency instances for several of the graphs\nconstructed in Constructions. We now provide two more, for the box\nproduct G □ H and for the single-edge graph edge s t (replacing Mathlib's\ninstance), and use them to explain why rw can be harmless or harmful inside a\nDecidable instance.\n\nIn order to decide if (g₁, h₁) is adjacent to (g₂, h₂) in G □ H we need to\ncheck if either g₁ = g₂ and H.Adj h₁ h₂, or h₁ = h₂ and G.Adj g₁ g₂. So we\nneed the obvious decidability assumptions on the vertex types of G and H as well\nas their adjacency relations.\n\nDecidability of the adjacency relation for the box product of two graphs./-- Decidability of the adjacency relation for the box product of two graphs. -/\ninstance boxProdDecidableRelAdj {V W : Type*} {G : SimpleGraph V} {H : SimpleGraph W}\n    [DecidableEq V] [DecidableEq W] [DecidableRel G.Adj] [DecidableRel H.Adj] :\n    DecidableRel (G □ H).Adj := by\n  intro a b; rw [boxProd_adj]; infer_instance\n\n\nThis instance uses rw to reduce adjacency in G □ H to a decidable proposition\nbefore calling infer_instance. This looks harmless, but can in some cases cause\nproblems.\n\nA proof by rw inserts a cast (an application of the recursor Eq.rec) into the\nterm it builds. Whether this matters depends on how the instance is used: #eval\nruns compiled code, from which all proofs, including the cast, have been erased, so\nevaluation is unaffected. But decide proves a proposition p by asking the kernel\nto reduce the Boolean decide p to true, which means first reducing the\nDecidable p instance far enough to read off its decide field. The kernel can only\nreduce an Eq.rec cast when the equation being cast along holds by definition (so\nthat, by proof irrelevance, the proof might as well be rfl). Otherwise the cast is\nstuck, exactly as h ▸ v was in the disjoint union Σg (SimpleGraph.sigma) in\nSum and Sigma Graphs before we substituted i = j.\n\nFor the box product instance, we got away with it: boxProd_adj is definitional\n(proved by Iff.rfl), so the cast vanishes during kernel reduction.\n\nexample {V W : Type*} {G : SimpleGraph V} {H : SimpleGraph W} {x y : V × W} :\n    (G □ H).Adj x y ↔ (G.Adj x.1 y.1 ∧ x.2 = y.2) ∨ (H.Adj x.2 y.2 ∧ x.1 = y.1) :=\n  Iff.rfl\n\n\nexample : (K[2] □ K[3]).Adj (0, 0) (0, 1) := by decide\n\n\nMathlib's instance for the single-edge graph edge s t is not so lucky. Since edge\nis defined using fromEdgeSet, the rewrite lemma edge_adj is a genuine theorem,\nnot provable by Iff.rfl, yet Mathlib's instance uses the same rw recipe.\n\n#source instDecidableRelAdjEdge -- instance : DecidableRel (edge s t).Adj :=\n                                --   fun _ _ ↦ by rw [edge_adj]; infer_instance\n\n\nWith this instance, #eval works fine.\n\n#eval (edge 0 2).Adj 0 2 -- true\n\n\nBut the tactic decide gets stuck on the cast. Note the ⋯ ▸ inferInstance in the\nerror message below: ▸ is the cast inserted by rw.This is the instance in\nthe version of Mathlib used by this guide, and may well be fixed in later\nversions!\n\n/- error: Tactic decide failed to reduce\n  @decide ((edge 0 2).Adj 0 2) (instDecidableRelAdjEdge 0 2 0 2)\nto true or false.\n\nAfter unfolding the instance instDecidableRelAdjEdge, reduction got stuck at\n  ⋯ ▸ inferInstance\n\nHint: Reduction got stuck on ▸ (Eq.rec), which suggests that one of the Decidable\ninstances is defined using tactics such as rw or simp. To avoid tactics, make use\nof functions such as «inferInstanceAs» or decidable_of_decidable_of_iff to alter\na proposition.\n-/\nexample : (edge 0 2).Adj 0 2 := by decide -- fails\n\n\nSo how should we fix this? The error message tells us what will work here: use\ndecidable_of_decidable_of_iff.\n\n#source decidable_of_decidable_of_iff -- .. (h : p ↔ q) : Decidable q where\n                                      --      decide := decide p\n                                      --      reflects_decide := ..\n\n\nNote that this isn't a theorem, it is a function that provides a Decidable instance\nfor q using a Decidable instance for p and a proof of p ↔ q. Its Boolean is\nsimply decide p: it asks the instance for p for its verdict and returns the same\nverdict for q. The proof h : p ↔ q is used only in the reflects_decide field,\nto turn the proof that this verdict is correct for p into one for q.\n\nThe crucial difference from the rw version is where h ends up. The rw version\ncasts the whole instance along h, so the kernel has to get past the cast before\nit can read off the decide field. Here h appears only inside reflects_decide,\nwhich is a proof that decide never needs to reduce. The decide field itself\ncontains no Eq.rec cast, so nothing blocks kernel reduction.\n\nLean tries the most recently declared instances first, so from now our instance given\nbelow takes priority over Mathlib's.\n\nDecidability of the adjacency relation for the single-edge graph edge s t./-- Decidability of the adjacency relation for the single-edge graph edge s t. -/\ninstance edgeDecidableRelAdj {V : Type*} [DecidableEq V] (s t : V) :\n    DecidableRel (edge s t).Adj := fun v w =>\n  decidable_of_decidable_of_iff (edge_adj s t v w).symm\n\n\nNow both #eval and decide work fine!\n\n#eval (edge (0 : Fin 5) 2).Adj 0 2 -- true\nexample : (edge 0  2).Adj 0 2 := by decide\nexample : ¬ (edge 0 2).Adj 1 2 := by decide\n\n\nIn practice we rarely call decidable_of_decidable_of_iff directly:\ndecidable_of_iff a h is the same function with the proposition a given\nexplicitly, and decidable_of_iff' b h takes h : a ↔ b the other way round. We\nused decidable_of_iff for pathNatFromEdgeSet above.\n\n#check decidable_of_iff  -- (a : Prop) (h : a ↔ b) [Decidable a] : Decidable b\n#check decidable_of_iff' -- (b : Prop) (h : a ↔ b) [Decidable b] : Decidable a\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Decidability, Finiteness and Degrees\u0009Decidability\u0009Deciding Adjacency","header":"4.1.4.1. Decidability and Kernel Reduction*","id":"/Decidability___-Finiteness-and-Degrees/#section-decidability-and-kernel-reduction"},"/Maps/#section-hypercubes-and-mycielskians":{"contents":"We now introduce the natural maps associated with hypercubes and Mycielskians.\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Maps","header":"5.5. Hypercubes and Mycielskians","id":"/Maps/#section-hypercubes-and-mycielskians"},"/SimpleGraph/#section-edges":{"contents":"Since SimpleGraph is a loopless undirected graph, its edges are unordered pairs\nof distinct vertices. In Lean unordered pairs have type Sym2 V, the symmetric\nsquare of V. Formally this is the quotient of V × V modulo swaps (Sym2.Rel).\nElements (or more precisely terms) of Sym2 V are denoted s(u, v). Note that\nalthough s(v, v) cannot be an edge of a SimpleGraph (which is loopless), the\ntype Sym2 V still contains such pairs.\n\nexample {u v : V} : s(u, v) = s(v, u) := Sym2.eq_swap\n\n\nThe edge set of G is G.edgeSet and is a Set (Sym2 V). Membership is\ndefinitionally equivalent to adjacency. (This means that the proof is rfl.)\n\nexample {u v : V} : s(u, v) ∈ G.edgeSet ↔ G.Adj u v := by rfl\n\n\nHowever using G.Adj u v has a couple of advantages over s(u, v) ∈ G.edgeSet: it\navoids the need for Sym2 V and, via dot notation, we have easy access to convenient\nlemmas about adjacency.\n\n#check Adj.ne -- G.Adj a b → a ≠ b\nexample {u v : V} (h : G.Adj u v) : u ≠ v := h.ne\nexample {u v : V} (h : G.Adj u v) : v ≠ u := h.ne'\n\n\nGenerally we prefer to use G.Adj u v rather than s(u, v) ∈ G.edgeSet when talking\nabout individual edges, but when we want to talk about a set of (possible) edges of\na graph then s : Set (Sym2 V) is what we need.\n\nWhen we do need to prove something about an arbitrary e : Sym2 V, the usual first\nstep is induction e using Sym2.ind. Every term of Sym2 V is s(x, y) for some\nx y : V, and Sym2.ind reduces the goal to that case. For example, no edge of G\nis a loop. Mathlib calls a pair of the form s(v, v) diagonal (Sym2.IsDiag).\n\nexample (e : Sym2 V) (he : e ∈ G.edgeSet) : ¬ e.IsDiag := by\n  induction e using Sym2.ind with\n  | _ x y =>                 -- he : s(x, y) ∈ G.edgeSet ⊢ ¬s(x, y).IsDiag\n    rw [mem_edgeSet] at he\n    rw [Sym2.mk_isDiag_iff]\n    exact he.ne\n\n\nNotice that induction replaced e by s(x, y) in the hypothesis he as well as\nin the goal. This result is G.not_isDiag_of_mem_edgeSet in Mathlib.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009SimpleGraph","header":"2.2. Edges","id":"/SimpleGraph/#section-edges"}});